traceability.evidence:
Rule implementation: src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts
  - File header explicitly ties rule to this story and requirements:
    - `@story docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md`
    - `@req REQ-DEEP-PARSE - Parse comments and extract story/requirement metadata`
    - `@req REQ-DEEP-MATCH - Match @req annotations to story file requirements`
    - `@req REQ-DEEP-CACHE - Cache requirement IDs per story file for efficient validation`
    - `@req REQ-DEEP-PATH - Validate and resolve story file paths safely`
  - Core parsing and matching logic:
    - `loadAndCacheRequirements(...)` reads the referenced story file and extracts all requirement IDs using `const regex = /REQ-[A-Z0-9-]+/g;` and stores them in a `Map<string, Set>` cache (implements REQ-DEEP-PARSE and REQ-DEEP-CACHE).
    - `validateReqLine(...)` extracts the `@req` ID from each `@req` line and, if a storyPath is known, resolves story+requirements and calls `checkRequirementExists(...)`.
    - `checkRequirementExists(...)` reports a diagnostic when the requirement is not found:
      ```ts
      context.report({
        node: comment as any,
        messageId: "reqMissing",
        data: { reqId, storyPath },
      });
      ```
      This is the deep match behavior (REQ-DEEP-MATCH / REQ-DEEP-ERROR).
  - Requirement formats and sections:
    - `loadAndCacheRequirements` scans the entire markdown file content for any `REQ-[A-Z0-9-]+` substring, independent of section or markdown structure. This naturally supports:
      - Explicit IDs in prose or headings
      - Bullet lists (`- REQ-...`)
      - Numbered lists (`1. REQ-...`)
      - Acceptance criteria checkboxes (`- [ ] REQ-...`)
      (covers REQ-DEEP-FORMAT and REQ-DEEP-SECTION).
  - Caching behavior:
    - `reqCache: Map<string, Set>` is created once per-program in `programListener(context)` and passed through all helpers (`loadAndCacheRequirements`, `resolveStoryAndRequirements`, `processAllComments`).
    - `loadAndCacheRequirements` populates the cache for a given `resolvedStoryPath` only once; subsequent `@req` checks for the same story reuse the cached `Set` instead of re-reading the file (REQ-DEEP-CACHE).
  - Error handling and graceful behavior:
    - `validateAndResolveStoryPath(...)` enforces path safety (`..` and absolute paths) and reports `invalidPath` via `context.report` without throwing (REQ-DEEP-PATH, error handling for invalid paths).
    - `loadAndCacheRequirements(...)` wraps `fs.readFileSync` in `try/catch`; on any I/O or parsing failure it stores an empty `Set` instead of throwing, so linting continues gracefully even for malformed/missing story files:
      ```ts
      try {
        const content = fs.readFileSync(resolvedStoryPath, "utf8");
        // ... extract REQ IDs ...
        reqCache.set(resolvedStoryPath, found);
      } catch {
        reqCache.set(resolvedStoryPath, new Set());
      }
      ```
      This fulfills the "gracefully handles malformed story files, missing sections, or parsing errors" acceptance criterion.
    - `checkRequirementExists(...)` then cleanly reports `reqMissing` when an ID isn't in the (possibly empty) set (REQ-DEEP-ERROR).
  - User-facing error messages:
    - `meta.messages` in `valid-req-reference.ts` defines clear diagnostics:
      ```ts
      reqMissing: "Requirement '{{reqId}}' not found in '{{storyPath}}'",
      invalidPath: "Invalid story path '{{storyPath}}'",
      ```
      These give both the missing requirement ID and the story path, satisfying the "User Experience" and REQ-DEEP-ERROR criteria.
  - Integration with existing plugin wiring:
    - `src/index.ts` includes this rule in `RULE_NAMES` and severity mapping:
      ```ts
      const RULE_NAMES = [
        "require-story-annotation",
        "require-req-annotation",
        "require-branch-annotation",
        "valid-annotation-format",
        "valid-story-reference",
        "valid-req-reference",
      ] as const;
      ...
      const TRACEABILITY_RULE_SEVERITIES = {
        "traceability/require-story-annotation": "error",
        "traceability/require-req-annotation": "error",
        "traceability/require-branch-annotation": "error",
        "traceability/valid-annotation-format": "warn",
        "traceability/valid-story-reference": "error",
        "traceability/valid-req-reference": "error",
      };
      ```
    - This ensures the deep validation rule is part of the main plugin and participates in the recommended/strict configs alongside the file validation rule (`valid-story-reference`) and annotation-format rule.

Tests:
- tests/rules/valid-req-reference.test.ts
  - File header explicitly ties tests to this story and its requirements:
    ```ts
    /**
     * Tests for: docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md
     * @story docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md
     * @req REQ-DEEP-PARSE - Verify valid-req-reference rule enforces existing requirement content
     *
     * Additional coverage for error reporting behavior:
     * @story docs/stories/007.0-DEV-ERROR-REPORTING.story.md
     * @req REQ-ERROR-SPECIFIC - Verify requirement-level errors identify the exact missing requirement
     * @req REQ-ERROR-CONTEXT - Verify requirement-level errors include relevant story path context
     * @req REQ-ERROR-CONSISTENCY - Verify requirement-level error messages are consistent across cases
     */
    ```
  - Uses `RuleTester` to exercise the rule end-to-end:
    ```ts
    describe("Valid Req Reference Rule (Story 010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION)", () => {
      ruleTester.run("valid-req-reference", rule, {
        valid: [
          {
            name: "[REQ-DEEP-PARSE] valid requirement reference existing in story file",
            code: `// @story docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md\n// @req REQ-PLUGIN-STRUCTURE`,
          },
          {
            name: "[REQ-DEEP-BULLET] valid bullet list requirement existing in bullet story fixture",
            code: `// @story tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md\n// @req REQ-BULLET-LIST`,
          },
        ],
        invalid: [
          {
            name: "[REQ-DEEP-MATCH] missing requirement in story file",
            code: `// @story docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md\n// @req REQ-NON-EXISTENT`,
            errors: [
              {
                messageId: "reqMissing",
                data: {
                  reqId: "REQ-NON-EXISTENT",
                  storyPath: "docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md",
                },
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            name: "[REQ-DEEP-PARSE] disallow path traversal in story path",
            code: `// @story ../docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md\n// @req REQ-PLUGIN-STRUCTURE`,
            errors: [
              {
                messageId: "invalidPath",
                data: {
                  storyPath: "../docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md",
                },
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            name: "[REQ-DEEP-PARSE] disallow absolute path in story path",
            code: `// @story /absolute/path/docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md\n// @req REQ-PLUGIN-STRUCTURE`,
            errors: [
              {
                messageId: "invalidPath",
                data: {
                  storyPath:
                    "/absolute/path/docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md",
                },
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            name: "[REQ-DEEP-BULLET] missing bullet list requirement in bullet story fixture",
            code: `// @story tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md\n// @req REQ-MISSING-BULLET`,
            errors: [
              {
                messageId: "reqMissing",
                data: {
                  reqId: "REQ-MISSING-BULLET",
                  storyPath: "tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md",
                },
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      });
    });
    ```
  - These tests confirm:
    - Correct positive behavior when `@req` IDs exist in a real story file (Core Functionality).
    - Correct negative behavior and `reqMissing` error when IDs do not exist (REQ-DEEP-MATCH / REQ-DEEP-ERROR).
    - Handling of bullet-list formatted requirements via `tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md` (REQ-DEEP-FORMAT & REQ-DEEP-SECTION).
    - Path traversal and absolute path detection with `invalidPath` messages (REQ-DEEP-PATH and Error Handling).

- tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md
  - Demonstrates support for markdown bullet-style requirements:
    ```md
    ## Requirements

    - REQ-BULLET-LIST - a requirement in bullet list format
    - REQ-ANOTHER - another requirement
    ```
  - Combined with the valid/invalid tests above, this is concrete evidence of handling non-trivial story structures.

Documentation:
- docs/rules/valid-req-reference.md
  - Documents the rule and ties it to this story and requirements:
    ```md
    @story docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md
    @req REQ-DEEP-PARSE - Parse story files to extract requirement identifiers
    @req REQ-DEEP-MATCH - Validate `@req` references against story file content
    @req REQ-DEEP-PATH - Protect against path traversal in story paths
    ```
  - Describes behavior consistent with the story:
    - Verifies story file exists and is within project directory.
    - Parses story file to extract requirement IDs.
    - Ensures each `@req` matches an extracted requirement ID.
    - Reports `invalidPath` on traversal/absolute paths.
    - Reports `reqMissing` when requirement ID is not found.
  - Provides concrete correct/incorrect examples, fulfilling the "Documentation" acceptance criterion.

